/* Pass 20B: exact canonical Fallout 4 and Harry Potter chapter revisions. */
(function(){'use strict';const lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;const changes={"what-the-armor-leaves-behind":{"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-08":{"number":8,"title":"Off the Record","content":"Danse asked to speak to me off the record.\n\nThis was funny because I had never once seen him write anything on a record while talking to me.\n\nI said so.\n\nHe gave me the look.\n\nBy then I had categorized several Danse looks.\n\nThere was the command look, which meant someone was about to receive an instruction whether they wanted one or not. The tactical look meant he had noticed danger before everyone else. The disappointed look meant Rhys had done something reckless. The particularly rare soft look appeared whenever Haylen fixed something nobody thought could be fixed.\n\nThe one he gave me now meant I was making a serious conversation difficult on purpose.\n\nI stopped smiling.\n\n“Sorry.”\n\nHe glanced toward the main room.\n\nHaylen and Rhys were arguing about inventory. Neither was paying attention to us.\n\nDanse led me into the back office and shut the door.\n\n“This isn’t disciplinary,” he said.\n\n“I wasn’t worried until you clarified.”\n\nHe sighed.\n\n“I wanted to apologize.”\n\nThat got my attention.\n\n“For?”\n\n“When we met, I expected you to operate by Brotherhood standards without giving you any reason to understand them.”\n\nI stared.\n\n“You’ve been thinking about this?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“How long?”\n\n“That’s not relevant.”\n\n“Meaning long enough to be embarrassing.”\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“I’m trying to say something.”\n\nI lifted both hands.\n\n“All right. Go ahead.”\n\nHe stood in front of the desk like it was a briefing.\n\nThen nothing came out.\n\nDanse looked toward the window. Back at me. Toward the floor.\n\nI realized with a jolt that he was nervous.\n\nPaladin Danse, who had stood under a rocket engine and ordered me to fire it, was frightened of a personal conversation.\n\nThe discovery did not make me want to laugh.\n\nIt made me want to be careful.\n\n“You don’t owe me a speech,” I said.\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You can just talk.”\n\n“I am talking.”\n\n“Technically.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nThen the tension in his shoulders eased a fraction.\n\n“I grew up in the Capital Wasteland,” he said. “Orphaned. Mostly scavenged ruins, sold what I could find.”\n\nI stayed quiet.\n\n“When I was older, I saved enough caps to set up a junk stand in Rivet City. That’s where I met Cutler.”\n\nThe name was new.\n\nHis voice changed around it.\n\n“We watched each other’s backs. Kept each other out of trouble. When the Brotherhood started recruiting, we joined together.”\n\n“Best friend?”\n\nDanse looked uncomfortable with the phrase.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI sat on the edge of the desk.\n\n“What happened?”\n\nHe breathed in slowly.\n\n“About a year after we were posted to the Prydwen, Cutler’s team disappeared.”\n\n“Prydwen?”\n\n“Our airship.”\n\n“You have an airship.”\n\n“That’s what you took from the sentence?”\n\n“I’ll be impressed later.”\n\nHe shook his head, but the moment of humor helped.\n\n“I convinced my commanding officer to authorize a search. Took three weeks. We finally tracked them to a super mutant nest.”\n\nHis hands closed into fists at his sides.\n\n“The others were dead. Cutler wasn’t.”\n\nI knew before he said it.\n\n“The mutants had exposed him to FEV. He was changing.”\n\nThe room felt smaller.\n\n“What did you do?”\n\nDanse looked directly at me.\n\n“I killed him.”\n\nThere was no attempt to soften it.\n\nNo mercy-kill language. No duty.\n\nJust the fact.\n\nI swallowed.\n\n“I’m sorry.”\n\n“Everyone says that.”\n\n“What should they say?”\n\n“I don’t know.”\n\nHis anger flickered, then collapsed into something older.\n\n“I told myself there wasn’t a choice. Brotherhood protocol was clear. FEV transformation is irreversible. Super mutants are dangerous.”\n\n“You still think that.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“But?”\n\nHe looked away.\n\n“I don’t know if he understood what was happening. I don’t know how much of him was still there when I…”\n\nHis voice stopped.\n\nThis was not doctrine anymore.\n\nIt was grief.\n\nI stood but did not move closer.\n\n“Did he attack you?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Did he ask you to do it?”\n\n“No.”\n\nDanse’s face hardened as if he expected judgment.\n\nI did not give him absolution either.\n\n“That sounds like something you may never know was right.”\n\nHis eyes met mine.\n\nThe tension in the room changed.\n\nMost people, I realized, probably told him he had done what was necessary.\n\nBrotherhood people certainly did.\n\n“You think I was wrong.”\n\n“I think you loved your friend and had seconds to make a decision no one should have to make.”\n\n“That isn’t an answer.”\n\n“No.”\n\nHe stared at me.\n\nThen, slowly, some of the anger went out of him.\n\n“Fair.”\n\nI waited.\n\n“After Cutler,” he said, “I stopped getting close to people.”\n\nI thought of the dead lockers.\n\n“Because soldiers die.”\n\n“Everyone dies.”\n\n“That’s cheerful.”\n\n“It’s true.”\n\n“And distance fixes that?”\n\n“No.”\n\nThe answer came so quietly I almost missed it.\n\nHe leaned against the desk beside me.\n\n“It just makes the loss less…”\n\n“Personal?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI looked at his profile.\n\nThe strong nose. Dark stubble. A small line between his brows that seemed permanently carved there by responsibility.\n\n“And how’s that working?”\n\nHe glanced toward the door where Haylen’s voice rose in irritation at Rhys.\n\n“Poorly.”\n\nI smiled.\n\nDanse did too.\n\nThis one lasted.\n\nThen he looked at me and the smile faded into something more uncertain.\n\n“You’re becoming difficult to categorize.”\n\n“That sounds serious.”\n\n“It is.”\n\n“What are the categories?”\n\n“Civilian. Contractor. Potential recruit.”\n\n“And?”\n\nHe hesitated.\n\n“Friend.”\n\nThe word landed with more force than it deserved.\n\nI knew then what it cost him.\n\nNot because he had said it dramatically.\n\nBecause he had not.\n\nDanse treated friendship like ammunition: valuable, limited, not spent carelessly.\n\nI bumped my shoulder lightly against his.\n\n“Careful, Paladin.”\n\nHe looked down at the point of contact.\n\n“About what?”\n\n“You’re getting sentimental.”\n\n“I am not.”\n\n“Off the record?”\n\nHe groaned.\n\nIt was the closest thing to laughter I had heard from him.\n\nWhen we left the office, Haylen looked between us with open curiosity.\n\nRhys scowled as a matter of principle.\n\nDanse returned immediately to checking supply manifests as if he had not just handed me one of the most painful pieces of himself.\n\nI picked up Righteous Authority and went to clean it.\n\nThe rifle had been his.\n\nThe story had been his.\n\nThe trust, somehow, was mine now too.\n\nI handled both carefully.\n\nHe tested the trust two days later without meaning to.\n\nA Brotherhood distress code came through the repaired array at dawn—faint, fragmented, probably old. Danse was out of his bunk before the second burst finished.\n\nSo was I.\n\nRhys began checking weapons. Haylen isolated the frequency.\n\nDanse gave orders with machine precision until he reached me.\n\n“You’re staying here.”\n\n“No.”\n\nHis head snapped toward me.\n\n“This is a Brotherhood matter.”\n\n“Yesterday ArcJet was a Brotherhood matter.”\n\n“That was different.”\n\n“How?”\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\n“You had been contracted for a specific objective.”\n\n“And today?”\n\n“Today I don’t know what we’re walking into.”\n\nI understood before he finished.\n\nNot policy.\n\nFear.\n\nHe had told me about losing people. Now there was a chance to lose another, and his solution was to remove the variable before it could hurt him.\n\n“You don’t get to tell me something painful and then start deciding my risks for me.”\n\nThe station went very quiet.\n\nRhys became intensely interested in the same rifle part for almost a full minute.\n\nDanse lowered his voice.\n\n“We’ll discuss this outside.”\n\n“Excellent idea.”\n\nWe stepped onto the cracked front steps.\n\nMorning fog hung over Cambridge.\n\nDanse turned on me.\n\n“You are not a member of my unit.”\n\n“Correct.”\n\n“I am not obligated to bring you into danger.”\n\n“Also correct.”\n\n“Then what exactly is the problem?”\n\n“The part where you pretend that means you’re entitled to keep me out of it.”\n\nHe stared.\n\nI softened my voice.\n\n“Danse. I heard what you said about losing people. I’m not making fun of it. I’m not treating it lightly.”\n\nHis anger thinned.\n\n“But I am not one of your dead soldiers waiting to happen.”\n\nHis face closed.\n\n“That is not what I said.”\n\n“No. It’s what you’re doing.”\n\nWind moved through the ruined street.\n\nHis eyes went past me toward the barricades where I had first met him.\n\n“I make decisions based on risk.”\n\n“So do I.”\n\n“And if yours is wrong?”\n\n“Then it’s mine.”\n\nHe looked back at me.\n\nThat landed harder than anything else.\n\nThe Brotherhood had given him responsibility for lives. The Commonwealth had taught me ownership of mine. Neither instinct was evil.\n\nThey were simply going to collide.\n\nFinally Danse exhaled.\n\n“Stay on my right flank.”\n\nI smiled.\n\n“Six feet?”\n\n“Approximately.”\n\n“Look at us. Compromise.”\n\n“Do not make me regret it.”\n\n“Impossible. You regret everything in advance.”\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\nWe went back inside to gear up.\n\nAt the door he stopped me with one hand on my shoulder.\n\n“If I order a retreat—”\n\n“I retreat.”\n\n“Immediately.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe studied my face as if checking for sarcasm.\n\nThere was none.\n\n“Thank you.”\n\nThe transmitter was dead, exactly as Danse had predicted. The three ferals inside the relay station were not.\n\nHe signaled for the north entrance without speaking. I took the position we'd agreed on, six feet to his right, where I could see the service corridor without blocking his firing line. The first ghoul struck a cabinet and knocked a tray of old ceramic insulators onto the concrete. The crash brought another from a room we hadn't inspected.\n\n“Two contacts,” I called.\n\n“Three. Far door,” Danse answered.\n\nI saw the third shape through the broken glass, moving toward the stairwell rather than us. There was a frightened scavenger halfway down the steps, caught between turning back and running. Danse turned his armor to shield the opening.\n\n“Withdrawal route, east,” he ordered. “Jasper, cover the door. The civilian goes first.”\n\nI didn't argue about the word *civilian*. I covered the corridor and guided the scavenger past the broken electrical cabinet. A shard of metal caught my sleeve, leaving a narrow tear. Danse saw it and went rigid.\n\n“Are you hit?”\n\n“No. Cloth.”\n\n“Confirm.”\n\nI showed him my arm without setting down the rifle. He looked at the clean skin, then nodded once. We finished the retreat without anyone playing hero. The ghouls stayed behind the reinforced door. There was no technology left to recover, and Danse logged the site as unsafe for unarmored entry.\n\nOutside, the scavenger tried to thank him. Danse asked whether they had somewhere safe to go. When they said yes, he gave them the approved route to the settlement and made no promises about a Brotherhood escort he hadn't been authorized to provide.\n\nI waited until they left.\n\n“You wanted to order me behind you.”\n\n“Yes,” he said.\n\n“You didn't.”\n\n“The position was yours.”\n\n“And the order to retreat?”\n\n“Was mine. You followed it.”\n\nI looked at the torn sleeve, then at him. The agreement had survived danger, which was more than our conversation in a quiet office could have proved.\n\n\n\nThe argument mattered anyway.\n\nIt was the first time Danse let me choose danger after he had admitted I mattered enough for the choice to frighten him.\n","word_count":1838},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-18":{"number":18,"title":"The Weight of Command","content":"Haylen broke her wrist because a floor collapsed beneath her.\n\nDanse treated it like a personal moral failure.\n\nThe mission had been routine: recover a cache of technical documents from an old municipal building. Haylen came because half the files were likely to be useless without a scribe. Rhys stayed on the Prydwen, which meant nobody complained about me for almost four hours.\n\nThen the third-floor hallway gave way.\n\nHaylen dropped through rotten concrete with a shout.\n\nDanse moved before I did.\n\nHe caught the edge, jumped down after her, and disappeared into dust.\n\n“Danse!”\n\n“I’m fine!”\n\nHaylen swore below.\n\n“She’s not!”\n\nI found the stairs.\n\nBy the time I reached them, Danse had Haylen sitting against the wall while he examined her arm. Her wrist was already swelling.\n\n“Could be worse,” she said.\n\nDanse’s face said it could not.\n\n“We’re aborting.”\n\n“Paladin, the files—”\n\n“Are not worth further injury.”\n\nHaylen looked at me.\n\nI shrugged.\n\n“He’s right.”\n\n“Traitor.”\n\nDanse splinted the wrist with careful hands.\n\nOn the trip back, he barely spoke.\n\nHaylen noticed too.\n\nAt the landing pad she pulled me aside while a medic took over.\n\n“Talk to him.”\n\n“Why me?”\n\nShe gave me a look.\n\n“Oh, come on.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“Nothing.” Her smile was infuriating. “He listens to you.”\n\n“Sometimes.”\n\n“More than anyone.”\n\nBefore I could interrogate that statement, the medic called her away.\n\nI found Danse in the armor bay checking the same buckle three times.\n\n“You know her wrist will heal.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You know the collapse wasn’t predictable.”\n\n“I should have checked the structure.”\n\n“With what? Your structural-collapse vision?”\n\nHe glared.\n\n“I’m serious.”\n\n“So am I.”\n\nDanse turned away.\n\n“Four soldiers are dead. Rhys was wounded. Now Haylen—”\n\n“Has a broken wrist.”\n\n“Because I put her in that building.”\n\n“She volunteered for the mission.”\n\n“I approved it.”\n\nI stepped into his line of sight.\n\n“You cannot command people and also own every bad thing that happens to them.”\n\n“Responsibility doesn’t disappear because it’s uncomfortable.”\n\n“Neither does everyone else’s agency.”\n\nHe went still.\n\nI continued before courage abandoned me.\n\n“You want everyone around you to be competent. You train them, trust them with weapons, expect them to make decisions. Then the second something goes wrong, you decide they were actually helpless pieces you moved into danger.”\n\nHis face hardened.\n\n“That’s not what I’m doing.”\n\n“It is.”\n\n“You think this is about control?”\n\n“I think it’s about grief.”\n\nThe word hit.\n\nDanse looked away.\n\n“You lost Cutler and Gladius. If every loss is your fault, then at least losses have a reason.”\n\n“That doesn’t make sense.”\n\n“Emotionally? It does.”\n\nHe paced once across the bay.\n\n“What would you have me do?”\n\n“Ask Haylen.”\n\n“Ask her what?”\n\n“Whether she blames you.”\n\n“She’ll say no.”\n\n“Then believe her.”\n\n“That’s not that simple.”\n\n“No. It’s harder.”\n\nHe stopped.\n\nFor a long time neither of us spoke.\n\nThen Danse sat heavily on a crate.\n\n“I don’t know how to do this.”\n\n“What?”\n\n“Care about people without…”\n\nHe trailed off.\n\n“Without preparing to lose them?”\n\nHis eyes lifted to mine.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nI sat beside him.\n\nOur shoulders touched.\n\n“You don’t.”\n\n“That’s not helpful.”\n\n“I mean you can’t prepare enough to make it not hurt.”\n\nHis jaw worked.\n\n“That’s a terrible system.”\n\n“I didn’t design it.”\n\n“Whoever did should be court-martialed.”\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe smiled weakly.\n\nThen his face sobered.\n\n“It’s a good feeling,” he said. “Having people you can trust. But it frightens me.”\n\nI thought of Cutler. Brach. Worwick. Dawes. Keane.\n\n“Because losing them changes you.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHis hand lay open on the crate between us.\n\nI put mine over it.\n\nDanse turned his palm and laced our fingers.\n\nThis time there was no excuse.\n\nNo narrow corridor, no injured hand, no off-the-record joke.\n\nHe chose it.\n\nMy heartbeat became embarrassingly loud.\n\nDanse stared straight ahead.\n\n“So,” I said softly, “is this appropriate, soldier?”\n\nHe looked at me.\n\nIt took him a second to recognize the callback.\n\nThen color rose in his face.\n\n“Don’t ruin it.”\n\nI smiled.\n\n“Wouldn’t dream of it.”\n\nWe sat hand in hand in the armor bay until Haylen appeared in the doorway wearing a brace.\n\nShe stopped.\n\nWe stopped.\n\nNobody moved.\n\nHaylen’s smile became enormous.\n\nDanse released my hand so quickly he nearly knocked a wrench to the floor.\n\n“Scribe.”\n\n“Paladin.”\n\n“How is the wrist?”\n\n“Broken.”\n\n“Yes. I’m aware.”\n\n“Medic says I’ll live.”\n\n“Good.”\n\nShe looked at me.\n\nThen at him.\n\nThen back at me.\n\n“I’ll leave you two to discuss… command responsibility.”\n\nDanse looked like he wanted the Prydwen to crash.\n\nI waited until she was gone.\n\nThen I laughed until my ribs hurt.\n\nHe buried his face in one hand.\n\n“This is why fraternization policies exist.”\n\n“We are not fraternizing.”\n\nHe looked at me.\n\nI looked at the hand he had just been holding.\n\n“Right,” I said. “Obviously.”\n\nHe groaned.\n\nThe sound followed me all the way down the corridor.\n\nHaylen's injury changed the rhythm of the station for a week, and she had opinions about every change.\n\nRhys tried to pull double duty. Danse began assigning checks every hour as though another inspection could reverse a collapsing floor. I watched Haylen sit through two days of people discussing her wrist while she was in the room.\n\nFinally she held up her uninjured hand.\n\n“I need one person to ask what I can still do,” she said. “Not three people deciding I'm furniture.”\n\nDanse stopped beside the inventory board. “Your medical restrictions—”\n\n“Are written right here.” She tapped the paper. “No heavy lift. No recoil. I can still catalog signal equipment and review salvage scans. I am the scribe. You hired me for the part of the job that requires reading.”\n\nRhys snorted. Danse's jaw tightened, then loosened.\n\n“Understood, Scribe,” he said.\n\nShe wasn't asking him to stop worrying. She wanted him to stop converting worry into a command structure built around her body. The distinction seemed to cost him something.\n\nAt the workbench, Haylen discovered that a crate of recovered circuit boards had been mislabeled by a temporary clerk. She checked the serial numbers, found the missing inventory slip and corrected it herself. The repair took an hour; no one needed a heroic rescue.\n\nLater I found Danse adjusting the roster.\n\n“You've put her on all the desk shifts,” I said.\n\n“They're within her restrictions.”\n\n“Did she agree?”\n\nHis pencil stopped. We both stared at the schedule.\n\nHe took it to Haylen. She chose two shifts and asked for the third to be given to the scribe who'd already volunteered. Danse made the change without pretending it was his idea.\n\nWhen we walked away, his hand caught mine briefly beneath the corridor lights.\n\n“You're about to say something,” he muttered.\n\n“Only that you took orders from a woman with a broken wrist.”\n\n“She issued a correction,” he said. “And it was valid.”\n\nThat was his version of a compliment. I squeezed his fingers before he could file it under professional conduct.\n\n\n\nRhys tried to compensate by doing twice as much and complaining three times as loudly. Haylen attempted to work one-handed until Danse confiscated a toolbox from her.\n\n“You’re impossible,” she told him.\n\n“You are on medical restriction.”\n\n“I can use a terminal with my other hand.”\n\n“You can rest.”\n\n“I can do both.”\n\n“No.”\n\nI watched from the doorway.\n\n“Does this sound familiar to anyone else?”\n\nBoth of them turned toward me.\n\nDanse’s eyes narrowed.\n\nHaylen smiled sweetly.\n\n“I have no idea what you mean.”\n\nLater, Danse and I walked the outer perimeter.\n\n“You’re hovering,” I said.\n\n“I am supervising.”\n\n“You checked her splint four times.”\n\n“Improper stabilization can cause complications.”\n\n“Doctor Danse.”\n\n“I have field medical training.”\n\n“Mother hen Danse.”\n\nHe stopped walking.\n\n“I strongly object to that description.”\n\n“That’s why it’s good.”\n\nHe resumed with offended dignity.\n\nAt the far barricade, a collapsed section of fencing had to be reset. We worked together without much conversation—me holding the post while he wired it into place, both of us falling into the easy coordination we had built on missions.\n\nWhen it was secure, Danse remained crouched.\n\n“I should have noticed the floor at the warehouse was unstable.”\n\nThere it was.\n\nAgain.\n\nThe endless ledger in his head.\n\n“You were twenty feet away.”\n\n“I assigned the sweep pattern.”\n\n“Haylen stepped where the floor broke.”\n\n“Under my orders.”\n\nI sat on the barrier beside him.\n\n“Do you believe she has agency?”\n\nHe frowned.\n\n“Of course.”\n\n“Then why does it disappear whenever something goes wrong?”\n\nHis face hardened.\n\n“That is not what I’m doing.”\n\n“You take credit for everyone’s mistakes and none of their choices.”\n\n“I take responsibility.”\n\n“Those are not the same thing.”\n\nDanse stood.\n\n“Command requires accepting consequences.”\n\n“Yes. It does not require imagining yourself as the sole cause of reality.”\n\nHe looked away.\n\nI softened my voice.\n\n“You know what Haylen said when I helped reset the splint?”\n\n“What?”\n\n“She said, ‘Don’t tell Danse, but I was showing off.’”\n\nHis head snapped back.\n\n“She what?”\n\n“She tried to jump a gap instead of walking around because Rhys said she couldn’t.”\n\nDanse stared toward the station.\n\nHis expression moved through disbelief, irritation, and exhausted affection.\n\n“I am going to kill both of them.”\n\n“Excellent leadership.”\n\nHe rubbed a hand over his face.\n\nThen he laughed.\n\n“I spent three days reviewing the route.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“The floor was not my fault.”\n\n“No.”\n\nHaylen recovered quickly enough to become annoyed by Danse checking on her.\n\nOn the third visit she finally held up her uninjured hand.\n\n“Paladin. I am medically cleared for light duty.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You read the report.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Twice.”\n\nHe did not answer.\n\nHaylen looked at me for help.\n\nI was a coward and looked at the ceiling.\n\n“Danse,” she said more gently, “the floor collapsed. You didn’t order it to.”\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\n“I selected the route.”\n\n“And I stepped where I stepped.”\n\n“You were following me.”\n\n“I also have eyes.”\n\nDanse looked genuinely offended by the suggestion that a subordinate might possess independent responsibility.\n\nHaylen sighed. “You know what actually happened?”\n\n“What?”\n\nShe grinned.\n\n“I was showing off.”\n\nHe stared at her.\n\nI finally looked down because I knew exactly where this was going.\n\n“She was showing off.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Because Rhys challenged her.”\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHe exhaled.\n\n“I may still kill him.”\n\n“Reasonable.”\n\nHaylen caught me in the corridor afterward.\n\n“Thank you,” she said.\n\n“For what?”\n\n“For getting him to stop treating my wrist like a command review.”\n\n“I didn’t. You did.”\n\nShe smiled. “He listens to you differently.”\n\nThe observation warmed and frightened me at the same time.\n\n“Is that good?”\n\n“Usually.” She glanced toward the garage where Danse was pretending not to watch us. “Just remember he listens hardest when he thinks he failed someone.”\n\nI did remember.\n\nThat was why I refused to become another person he could only love through responsibility.\n\nWe started back toward the station.\n\nHalfway there his hand brushed mine.\n\nThen again.\n\nOn the third pass, he caught my fingers.\n\nI looked at him.\n\nDanse kept his eyes forward.\n\n“Perimeter morale,” he said.\n\n“Obviously.”\n\nWe walked the rest of the way holding hands like it was a tactical necessity.\n","word_count":1858},"what-the-armor-leaves-behind-42":{"number":42,"title":"Letter from the Old World","content":"The envelope was made from a page torn out of an old technical manual.\n\nIt arrived at Brasswatch Yard wrapped around a small packet of electrical connectors, delivered by a trader who did not know the sender's name. The paper had been folded so precisely that I could almost hear Danse correcting someone's technique.\n\nHe recognized the handwriting before he opened it.\n\nHis face went still.\n\n\"Haylen?\"\n\nI looked at the packet, then at him.\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nHe carried it into the workshop and shut the door. I stayed outside. It was not an act of generosity; it was an effort not to confuse my curiosity with his permission.\n\nFive minutes passed.\n\nThen ten.\n\nAt last, he opened the door.\n\n\"Jasper?\"\n\nI went inside.\n\nDanse stood beside the bench with a folded note in one hand. The packet of connectors lay unopened. His shoulders were set as if he expected an inspection.\n\n\"It's from Haylen,\" he said.\n\n\"Do you want to tell me what it says?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe handed me the paper.\n\nThe message was short. Haylen wrote that the Brotherhood had changed patrol routes near a settlement Danse once supplied. Some residents were afraid a former Paladin might try to use the settlement's old radio frequency. The letter did not ask Danse to return. It asked him to avoid the channel until the people who lived there had chosen a safer way to communicate.\n\nAt the end, in smaller writing, she'd added:\n\nI HOPE YOU ARE WELL. PLEASE DON'T ANSWER THROUGH THE OLD NET.\n\nI read it twice.\n\n\"She wants you safe,\" I said.\n\n\"She wants civilians safe.\"\n\n\"Both can be true.\"\n\nDanse did not answer.\n\nHe took the note and placed it beside the purifier ledger.\n\n\"The old net is the most reliable frequency I know.\"\n\n\"That doesn't make it the right one.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe sounded irritated.\n\n\"Danse.\"\n\n\"I know, Jasper.\"\n\nHe moved to the workbench and began sorting the connectors by size, though they were already sorted. The motion was familiar enough to make my chest ache.\n\nI waited until he stopped.\n\n\"What are you afraid of?\" I asked.\n\n\"That if I don't respond, she'll think I have forgotten her.\"\n\n\"And if you respond through the channel?\"\n\n\"People could be endangered.\"\n\n\"Then the answer isn't a radio message.\"\n\nHe looked at me, the conflict plain.\n\n\"She's one of the few people who knew me before everything changed.\"\n\nI stood beside him, close enough that our sleeves touched.\n\n\"You don't have to stop caring about her to respect the boundary she's giving you.\"\n\nHis breath caught.\n\n\"That's not what I meant.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe looked down at his hands.\n\n\"I was trained to answer a call. Silence meant failure. Sometimes abandonment.\"\n\n\"Then maybe you need a different way to show you're still here.\"\n\nHe thought about it.\n\nThe following morning, we went to Neri with the note. Not to ask permission to answer Haylen, but to ask whether the Threadway courier network could deliver a sealed letter without identifying the settlement or exposing the person who had carried the first message.\n\nNeri listened and shook their head.\n\n\"Not without the recipient agreeing to receive it.\"\n\nDanse's jaw tightened.\n\n\"Then how do people communicate across the doors?\"\n\n\"They arrange it,\" Neri said. \"Or they wait.\"\n\n\"Waiting is unreliable.\"\n\n\"Sometimes it is the only honest answer.\"\n\nHe looked as though he wanted to argue. Instead, he asked for a blank page.\n\nWe returned to Brasswatch.\n\nHe wrote the letter slowly. I did not read over his shoulder. When he finished, he folded it and placed it in a plain envelope with no identifying marks on the outside.\n\nHe told me what he'd written only after he sealed it.\n\nHe had thanked Haylen for the warning. He had said he would not use the old network. He had told her that his life was different, and that the people who once served beside him still mattered. He had not asked her to put herself at risk for a reply.\n\n\"And now?\" I asked.\n\n\"Now I wait until there's a safe invitation.\"\n\nHe looked almost ill from the effort.\n\nI found two cups and poured water from the safe hand pump.\n\n\"That sounds difficult.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\n\"Welcome to the club.\"\n\nHe took the cup.\n\n\"You make light of everything.\"\n\n\"Not everything. Just the parts that might crush us if we never laugh.\"\n\nA small smile reached his mouth.\n\nBefore we returned to the purifier, Renn found Danse studying the sealed letter in his desk drawer.\n\n“Don't use the old courier seal,” they warned. “The mark on the outside is enough to tell somebody which network carried it.”\n\nDanse looked up sharply. “You recognized it?”\n\n“I recognized that you were hiding the mark under your hand. Different thing.”\n\nHe didn't enjoy the distinction. His first instinct was to ask exactly how much Renn knew and whether the letter needed to be confiscated. I saw his fingers close around the envelope.\n\nRenn had not asked to become an intelligence officer. They were carrying a worn connector and trying to keep the workbench safe.\n\n“The return label can be ordinary,” they said. “Use a permitted merchant address if the sender consents. No reason to put anyone else at risk because you want an answer today.”\n\nDanse set the letter down.\n\n“I am not asking you to handle it,” he said.\n\n“I wasn't offering.”\n\nAfter Renn left, he looked at the blank envelope for a long moment.\n\n“I used to think an unanswered message was a failure to maintain contact.”\n\n“Sometimes it's somebody choosing the safest way to live.”\n\nHe nodded. He put the original in a locked personal drawer, not the workshop records, and wrote a note reminding himself to ask before forwarding any part of Haylen's words.\n\nThat evening he repaired the purifier's cracked gasket instead of drafting another letter. The water ran clear through the test line; Renn recorded the measurement and signed it.\n\nThere was still a letter in the drawer. There was also a repaired machine. He had no need to make one stand in for the other.\n\n\"Are we getting a new parts supplier?\" they asked.\n\n\"Not yet,\" he said.\n\nRenn held up the connector packet Haylen had sent. \"These might fit the old regulator. I wasn't going to open them without asking.\"\n\nDanse thanked them. They compared the connectors with the workshop's inventory, noting which ones could be safely tested and which would need a separate circuit check. He didn't use the parts merely because they had come from someone he trusted. A good source could still send the wrong component.\n\n\"Should we credit the sender in the public repairs ledger?\" Renn asked.\n\nDanse's hand paused.\n\n\"No personal name,\" he said. \"Record the specification and the inspection result. If the supplier ever wants to be publicly identified, that decision should be theirs.\"\n\nRenn nodded and wrote *private donation, pending test* instead. I saw a little relief move through Danse's shoulders.\n\nHe wasn't closing off Haylen's part in his life. He was keeping a correspondence safe until she could choose another way to receive it.\n\n\"You can miss somebody without transmitting on an unsafe channel,\" I said when Renn left.\n\n\"I am discovering that silence requires substantially more discipline than I was taught.\"\n\n\"Well, there's something they don't put on the recruitment poster.\"\n\nHe gave me a long look, then returned to the actual repair instructions. The connector remained on the bench until it could be tested. The letter stayed sealed.\n\nWe worked the rest of the day on the purifier's intake pipe. Severus had warned us about residues that appeared after the system sat idle. Danse had taken the criticism seriously enough to redesign the testing schedule. Every sample would be collected and labeled before anyone declared the water safe.\n\nAt noon, Tamsin stopped by with a list of residents who needed deliveries until the repairs were complete. Danse read the list carefully.\n\n\"Three households require assistance carrying water,\" he said.\n\n\"Four,\" I corrected. \"One person doesn't want their name announced publicly.\"\n\nHe looked up.\n\n\"Then we can arrange a route without displaying it.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\n\"You're learning.\"\n\n\"I was already capable of discretion.\"\n\n\"Understatement of the century.\"\n\nHe shook his head, but there was warmth in it.\n\nThe letter stayed in a locked drawer. Not because he had stopped wanting to send it, but because wanting didn't give him the right to use a channel that might harm someone else.\n\nThat evening he found me on the workshop steps.\n\n\"Could I ask you something?\" he said.\n\n\"Always.\"\n\nHe sat beside me, the old scarf loose around his neck.\n\n\"Do you think caring about someone far away makes what I have with you less important?\"\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"No. You know what I believe about loving more than one person. But even outside romance, care isn't a resource we use up by remembering people.\"\n\nHe nodded slowly.\n\n\"I still have trouble believing that.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Then perhaps I should say something I do believe.\" He looked directly at me. \"I want you here tonight. Not because Haylen isn't here. Because you are.\"\n\nWarmth settled behind my ribs.\n\n\"That I can do.\"\n\nHe touched my cheek with one hand, waiting for the small answering movement that told him I wanted the kiss. I leaned into his palm.\n\nHis mouth was warm. His beard scratched lightly against my skin. He was still learning how to be gentle without making gentleness feel like a protocol.\n\nWhen we parted, he kept his forehead against mine for a moment.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he said.\n\n\"For what?\"\n\n\"For not asking me to forget.\"\n\nI squeezed his hand.\n\nThe next morning, the letter was still in the drawer.\n\nThe purifier was still offline.\n\nBoth were unfinished.\n\nFor the first time, Danse seemed willing to let unfinished mean that he could keep caring without taking the wrong action simply to make the waiting stop.","word_count":1657}},"after-always":{"after-always-08":{"number":8,"title":"Spinner's End","content":"Three days later, Snape asked me to go home with him.\n\nHe phrased it as an order.\n\n“You will accompany me to Spinner's End tomorrow.”\n\nI looked up from a crate of shrivelfigs. “Will I?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Is this a kidnapping?”\n\n“If it were, I would have chosen someone less conversational.”\n\nHis voice had returned, though it remained rough and he rationed it more carefully. Madam Pomfrey had forbidden interviews for a week. Snape had celebrated by speaking only when necessary and using his restored voice primarily for insults.\n\n“What are we retrieving?” I asked.\n\n“Books. Personal ingredients. Several journals relevant to the evidence review.”\n\n“Why do you need me?”\n\nHe gave me a look.\n\nI waited.\n\nHis jaw tightened.\n\n“Because,” he said at last, “I am not permitted to carry more than fifteen pounds.”\n\nI tried very hard not to smile.\n\nHis eyes narrowed.\n\n“I see.”\n\n“You see nothing.”\n\n“Of course.”\n\n“And because the house may have been searched.”\n\nThat erased the humor.\n\n“Ministry?”\n\n“Or others.”\n\n“Then we go together.”\n\n“I had already established that.”\n\nThe next morning, we traveled by Floo to a public hearth in Cokeworth because the connection at his house had been damaged.\n\nThe town was gray even under summer light.\n\nRows of brick houses pressed against narrow streets. Old mill chimneys rose beyond rooftops like blackened fingers. The river smelled faintly of metal and rain.\n\nSnape wore Muggle clothes beneath a long black coat. Nothing elaborate. Trousers, boots, a white shirt buttoned high enough to cover his scar.\n\nWithout robes, he looked less like Professor Snape and more like a man who had forgotten how to live anywhere but inside his own defenses.\n\nHe caught me looking.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Nothing.”\n\n“You should retire that word.”\n\n“You'd miss it.”\n\n“I assure you—”\n\nHe stopped.\n\nAcross the street, two boys were throwing stones at a rusted sign.\n\nSnape's face had gone distant.\n\n“Did you grow up here?” I asked.\n\nThe question was relevant enough to the trip that I risked it.\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Do you want me to stop asking questions?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Fine.”\n\nHe looked almost suspicious of my compliance.\n\nSpinner's End was narrower than I expected. The houses crowded close together, soot-darkened and tired. Snape's stood near the end of the row with curtains drawn and paint peeling around the door.\n\nHe stopped at the gate.\n\nFor a moment, he did nothing.\n\nI understood that threshold better than I wanted to.\n\nA place could be yours and still require courage to enter.\n\n“You can wait outside,” he said.\n\n“I thought I was here to carry things.”\n\n“You are.”\n\n“Then I'll need access to the things.”\n\nHis mouth tightened.\n\nHe opened the gate.\n\nThe front door had been forced.\n\nSnape's wand was in his hand immediately.\n\nMine followed.\n\n“Behind me,” he said.\n\n“No.”\n\nHis head snapped toward me.\n\n“Beside you.”\n\n“This is not the time—”\n\n“Exactly.”\n\nHe looked furious.\n\nThen he shifted half a step, making room.\n\nWe entered together.\n\nThe house smelled of dust, old paper, and damp brick.\n\nThe sitting room had been searched. Drawers pulled out. Cushions cut open. Books scattered across the floor. A framed photograph lay face down near the hearth.\n\nSnape did not pick it up.\n\nHe moved through the room methodically, checking corners and doors.\n\nNo one remained.\n\nThe kitchen was worse.\n\nCabinets stood open. A jar of dried nettles had smashed across the tile. Someone had carved TRAITOR into the wooden table.\n\nSnape stared at the word.\n\nHis face went completely blank.\n\nI hated whoever had written it.\n\nNot because the word was entirely false.\n\nThat was the cruel part.\n\nHe had betrayed people. More than once. He had betrayed Lily's trust as a boy. He had betrayed the Order when he joined Voldemort. He had betrayed Voldemort when he turned spy. He had deceived colleagues, students, enemies, allies.\n\nThe insult had been chosen because it could attach itself to truth.\n\nI raised my wand.\n\nThe carved letters vanished beneath a repair charm.\n\nSnape looked at me.\n\n“I did not ask you to do that.”\n\n“I know.”\n\n“You should not alter potential evidence.”\n\n“I photographed it first.”\n\nI held up the small enchanted plate from my case.\n\nHis eyes dropped to it.\n\nThen back to the clean table.\n\n“Efficient,” he said.\n\nIt sounded like thank you.\n\nWe didn't go upstairs immediately.\n\nSeverus turned the enchanted photograph toward the light and asked me to explain exactly when I'd taken it. The plate held the word, the grain of the damaged wood, and the position of a broken jar on the floor. It did not hold the original cuts beneath my repair charm.\n\n“I should have stopped you,” he said.\n\n“I should have stopped myself.”\n\n“An unfamiliar development. I had almost begun to expect your impulsiveness to be a permanent feature of the furniture.”\n\nHis cruelty was familiar, but this time I could hear how frightened he was. Someone had searched his home. I had been angry on his behalf, and my anger had destroyed something that might have belonged to an investigation.\n\n“We can document the alteration,” I said.\n\n“We must.”\n\nHe found paper in a drawer that had escaped the search and dictated a brief account. He made me mark the time approximately, because I hadn't looked at a clock. Then he wrote beneath it that the original tabletop was no longer available for independent examination. His own signature went beside mine, witnessing what he'd seen after the charm.\n\n“Does that make it better?” I asked.\n\n“It makes the record less misleading.”\n\nI looked around at the smashed jars and scattered books. Nothing was fixed by the fact that we had written down our mistake. The room was still frightening to him. The evidence was still altered.\n\n“I'm sorry,” I said, without explaining why I had done it.\n\nHe set the page inside the photograph sleeve. “I believe you.”\n\nHe was still angry. He had every right to be. We took the stairs without touching, leaving room for both facts.\n\n\nUpstairs, his bedroom had been disturbed but not destroyed. A narrow bed stood against the wall. Shelves bowed beneath books. A desk faced a small window overlooking the backs of neighboring houses.\n\nThere was almost nothing decorative.\n\nNo trophies. No framed certificates. No evidence of twenty years at Hogwarts beyond a black teaching robe hanging behind the door.\n\nOn the desk sat a photograph of a thin, dark-haired woman with heavy brows.\n\nSnape crossed the room and turned it face down.\n\n“My mother,” he said before I could look away.\n\nI nodded.\n\nHe opened a cupboard.\n\nInside were jars of ingredients organized with absolute precision.\n\nI smiled.\n\n“What?”\n\n“Alphabetical.”\n\nHe froze.\n\nI pointed.\n\n“Asphodel. Belladonna. Chizpurfle carapace. Dittany.”\n\nHis face became offended.\n\n“This is a domestic cupboard.”\n\n“So alphabetization is morally acceptable at home.”\n\n“It is space-efficient here.”\n\n“I will be noting this in the audit.”\n\n“You will do no such thing.”\n\nThe tension eased by a fraction.\n\nWe packed journals into two cases. Some were school-era notes. Others documented brewing under Voldemort's orders, encoded in a system only Snape understood.\n\n“Why keep them?” I asked.\n\nHe paused.\n\n“Because destroying records does not destroy acts.”\n\nThat answer stayed with me.\n\nIn the back room, we found the personal ingredients he wanted: rare resins, preserved venom sacs, powdered stone, a sealed tin of something that hissed when moved.\n\n“What is that?” I asked.\n\n“Nothing you need.”\n\n“It hisses.”\n\n“So do I, on occasion.”\n\n“I've noticed.”\n\nBy late afternoon, the cases were full.\n\nSnape sat on the edge of the bed while I sealed them. He looked exhausted.\n\n“Five minutes,” I said.\n\n“I did not ask.”\n\n“Neither did your nervous system.”\n\nHe was too tired to argue properly.\n\nI sat on the desk chair.\n\nFor a while we listened to the street outside.\n\nA lorry passed.\n\nSomeone shouted from a garden.\n\nA radio played through an open window two houses away.\n\n“This is where you learned about magic?” I asked.\n\nHe looked at the floor.\n\n“I already knew of it. My mother was a witch.”\n\n“And your father?”\n\n“A Muggle.”\n\nThe word contained no contempt. Only weariness.\n\n“Did he know?”\n\n“Yes.”\n\n“Did he like it?”\n\nA humorless smile.\n\n“My father liked very little.”\n\nI knew better than to ask more.\n\nSnape's eyes moved to the window.\n\n“I used to watch for her from here.”\n\nI went still.\n\nHe did not say Lily's name.\n\nHe did not need to.\n\n“She lived nearby?”\n\n“Close enough.”\n\nHis voice had gone rough again, but this time I suspected the scar was not responsible.\n\n“I thought Hogwarts would solve everything,” he said.\n\nThe admission was so quiet I almost missed it.\n\n“Did it?”\n\nHe gave me a sidelong look.\n\n“Clearly.”\n\nI smiled despite myself.\n\nThen he added, “It gave me different problems.”\n\n“That tends to be what escape does.”\n\nHis gaze sharpened.\n\nI wondered if I had revealed too much.\n\nHe did not ask.\n\nI appreciated that.\n\nWhen the five minutes became ten, he stood.\n\nWe carried the cases downstairs together, though I took the heavier one before he could protest.\n\nAt the front door, he looked back into the house.\n\n“Are you keeping it?” I asked.\n\n“I have not decided.”\n\n“It doesn't seem to like you much.”\n\n“Houses do not have opinions.”\n\n“This one feels judgmental.”\n\n“It has had poor examples.”\n\nWe stepped outside.\n\nSnape locked the door with three spells.\n\nAs we walked toward the public Floo, rain began to fall.\n\nHe did not hurry.\n\nNeither did I.\n\nFor the first time since meeting him, I had seen the place where Severus Snape had begun.\n\nIt did not explain him.\n\nNothing could excuse every choice made by the adult because of pain suffered by the child.\n\nBut the gray streets, the narrow room, the turned-down photograph, the books stacked like escape routes—all of it added context where gossip had only supplied judgment.\n\nAt the corner, Snape glanced at me.\n\n“You are quiet.”\n\n“I can be.”\n\n“Rarely.”\n\n“Do you want me to say something?”\n\nHe considered.\n\n“No.”\n\nSo I walked beside him in the rain.\n\nThis time, silence did not feel like distance.","word_count":1687},"after-always-18":{"number":18,"title":"Hogsmeade in Rain","content":"Snape invited me to Hogsmeade by informing me that my boots were unacceptable.\n\n“They leak,” he said.\n\nI looked down.\n\nA dark patch of water had soaked through near the toe.\n\n“They are old.”\n\n“They are compromised.”\n\n“They are boots, not evidence.”\n\n“Everything becomes evidence when it leaves mud on my laboratory floor.”\n\nI glanced at the spotless stone behind me.\n\n“There is no mud.”\n\n“There will be.”\n\n“Is this your way of suggesting I buy new ones?”\n\n“It is my way of insisting you cease bringing damp leather near powdered ingredients.”\n\n“Very romantic.”\n\nThe word escaped before I could catch it.\n\nSnape froze.\n\nSo did I.\n\nRain tapped the narrow windows.\n\nHe recovered first.\n\n“Your standards for romance are alarming.”\n\nThere was no rejection in it.\n\nOnly dry deflection.\n\nMy heart behaved stupidly.\n\n“Fine,” I said. “Hogsmeade?”\n\n“I require supplies.”\n\n“Of course you do.”\n\n“And you require boots.”\n\n“Entirely professional.”\n\n“Painfully.”\n\nWe left after lunch.\n\nThe rain was steady rather than violent, silver across the grounds and soft against the lake. Snape carried an umbrella. I had never imagined him carrying an umbrella before and found the sight disproportionately charming.\n\nHe noticed me looking.\n\n“No.”\n\n“I didn't say anything.”\n\n“You were about to.”\n\n“I was about to compliment your umbrella.”\n\n“It is an umbrella.”\n\n“A very severe one.”\n\n“It is black.”\n\n“Exactly.”\n\nHe moved it slightly toward me as the wind changed.\n\nI pretended not to notice that either.\n\nHogsmeade was rebuilding in patches. Honeydukes had fresh glass in its windows. The Three Broomsticks had a new sign. Several buildings still showed scorch marks beneath repair charms.\n\nPeople noticed Snape.\n\nSome stared.\n\nSome whispered.\n\nOne man deliberately crossed the street.\n\nSnape's face hardened a little more each time.\n\nI hated that I could see it.\n\nWe entered a cobbler's shop near the post office.\n\nThe witch behind the counter recognized him and did not react visibly. I loved her immediately.\n\n“Need something waterproof,” I said.\n\nShe measured my feet with a charm.\n\nSnape wandered toward a shelf of polish.\n\n“You don't have to supervise,” I called.\n\n“I am not supervising.”\n\n“You're three feet away.”\n\n“Emotionally, miles,” he said.\n\nI stared at him.\n\nHe had stolen my line.\n\nHis mouth twitched.\n\n“Thief,” I said.\n\n“An accusation from the current possessor of my textbook.”\n\nThe cobbler looked between us and smiled carefully.\n\nI felt heat climb my face.\n\nSnape saw.\n\nThe bastard looked pleased.\n\nI bought boots.\n\nHe bought nothing.\n\n“Your turn,” I said when we stepped outside.\n\n“I do not require footwear.”\n\n“You said supplies.”\n\n“Apothecary.”\n\nWe went to the apothecary.\n\nThen the quill shop.\n\nThen, inexplicably, a secondhand bookshop where Snape spent forty minutes pretending not to enjoy himself.\n\nI found him in a back aisle holding a nineteenth-century text on venom stabilization.\n\n“You look happy,” I said.\n\nHe almost dropped the book.\n\n“I look literate.”\n\n“Very attractively.”\n\nThere.\n\nI did it on purpose this time.\n\nSnape stared at me.\n\nThe rain drummed on the roof.\n\nMy pulse hammered harder.\n\nI could have laughed it away.\n\nI did not.\n\nHis gaze moved over my face with uncomfortable thoroughness.\n\nThen he looked down at the book.\n\n“You are flirting.”\n\nIt was not a question.\n\n“Yes.”\n\nHis fingers tightened around the spine.\n\n“Why?”\n\nI blinked.\n\n“Because I find you attractive.”\n\nHe looked genuinely offended by the concept.\n\n“That is not an explanation.”\n\n“It usually is.”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Fine. Because you're brilliant and difficult and unexpectedly funny. Because you notice everything. Because you can make a potion recipe sound like a moral argument. Because you are terrible at accepting care and getting slightly less terrible at offering it.”\n\nHis face had gone very still.\n\nI heard my own voice and decided I had already ruined caution, so I continued.\n\n“Because I like your hands. Because your sarcasm makes me laugh when it isn't aimed at children. Because you looked absurdly pleased when my Patronus almost took shape.”\n\nSnape's throat moved.\n\n“And,” I added, “because apparently I have poor survival instincts.”\n\nHe stared at me for a long time.\n\nThen he said, “That last explanation has merit.”\n\nI laughed, partly from relief.\n\nHe put the venom book back on the shelf.\n\n“You do not know me well enough.”\n\n“For what?”\n\n“To make that assessment.”\n\n“Attraction doesn't require a tribunal.”\n\n“It should.”\n\n“That sounds exhausting.”\n\n“It is safer.”\n\nThere it was.\n\nNot disinterest.\n\nFear dressed as procedure.\n\nI leaned against the shelf.\n\n“You don't have to do anything with it.”\n\nHis eyes sharpened.\n\n“I mean it. I'm not asking for a relationship. I'm not asking for a confession. I'm telling you something true because pretending otherwise is becoming ridiculous.”\n\n“You expect nothing?”\n\n“I expect you not to punish me for honesty.”\n\nHis expression changed.\n\n“I would not.”\n\n“Good.”\n\n“And if I do not reciprocate?”\n\n“I'll survive.”\n\nHis mouth thinned.\n\n“You use that word carelessly.”\n\nI softened.\n\n“Fair.”\n\nThe bookseller coughed somewhere near the front.\n\nSnape looked toward the sound, then back at me.\n\n“I find your presence…”\n\nHe stopped.\n\nI waited.\n\nHis face tightened with obvious annoyance at language.\n\n“Less objectionable than most.”\n\nI laughed so suddenly I startled a stack of magazines.\n\nSnape looked offended.\n\n“That is the worst declaration of interest I have ever received.”\n\n“It was not a declaration.”\n\n“Then what was it?”\n\n“A regrettable lapse in judgment.”\n\n“Should I write it down?”\n\n“No.”\n\n“Frame it?”\n\n“Absolutely not.”\n\nI was still smiling when we left the shop.\n\nThe rain had intensified.\n\nWe ducked beneath the awning of a closed bakery.\n\nSnape opened the umbrella.\n\nIt was too small for two people unless we stood close.\n\nNeither of us commented on that.\n\nHis shoulder pressed lightly against mine as we walked.\n\nThe contact remained.\n\nAt the Three Broomsticks, we stopped for dinner because the path back to Hogwarts had flooded near the bridge.\n\nMadam Rosmerta greeted Snape with a cautious neutrality that warmed after he ordered soup and did not insult anyone.\n\nThe waitress set a spare cloth beside Severus's plate. The handle of the umbrella had rubbed his recovering hand raw, and she'd noticed the blood before either of us had. He looked offended by the intervention.\n\n“I am not dying,” he said.\n\n“Good,” she replied. “Then you won't mind if I keep blood off the clean tablecloth.”\n\nShe disappeared before he could construct a sufficiently elaborate reply. I tried not to smile. He pressed the cloth to his palm with the grim dignity of a man losing an argument to hospitality.\n\n“You can let me hold that next time,” I said.\n\n“I can also purchase a useful umbrella.”\n\n“We're getting closer to a real date every minute.”\n\nHe gave me a look that should have spoiled soup.\n\nI had not mistaken the way he stood beside me under the awning. Nor had I forgotten that I'd asked a man with an injured hand to share a handle meant for one person. He was able to want me and still be annoyed. I was able to care for him without turning every small injury into a heroic test.\n\n“Can I see?” I asked.\n\nHe held out his hand. The cut was shallow. I dabbed it once, careful not to linger when he pulled back.\n\n“There,” I said. “Nobody died. The drama survived.”\n\n“Your medical conclusions are appallingly broad.”\n\n“That's why I don't teach Potions.”\n\n“No. There are several reasons.”\n\nHe looked almost amused. Then he slid the spare cloth within my reach, an unspoken offer for me to dry my own rain-soaked sleeve. I took it without making him name the kindness. For a few minutes we ate soup while the rain struck the shutters; no one needed to decide the future of our relationship before dessert.\n\n\nWe took a corner table.\n\n“This is dangerously close to a date,” I said.\n\n“It is weather-related shelter.”\n\n“With food.”\n\n“Most shelters improve with food.”\n\n“And shopping.”\n\n“You purchased boots. I purchased nothing.”\n\n“You almost bought a venom book.”\n\n“I exercised restraint.”\n\n“Very seductive.”\n\nHe looked at me over his soup.\n\n“You are going to continue this.”\n\n“Probably.”\n\nA pause.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because you haven't asked me to stop.”\n\nHis eyes held mine.\n\n“No,” he said.\n\nThe word settled between us.\n\nNot a confession.\n\nNot a promise.\n\nPermission.\n\nWhen the rain eased, we walked back under one umbrella.\n\nHalfway up the path, Snape's hand brushed mine.\n\nThen, deliberately, his little finger hooked around mine for three steps.\n\nOnly three.\n\nAfter that he adjusted his grip on the umbrella as if nothing had happened.\n\nI said nothing.\n\nInside the apothecary, Severus spent twelve minutes pretending the outing had a professional purpose.\n\nHe inspected powdered asphodel. Rejected two jars of lacewing flies. Argued with the proprietor about storage humidity. I followed with the patience of someone watching a cat insist it had entered a room for entirely unrelated reasons.\n\n“You know,” I said when the proprietor disappeared into the back, “we could simply walk around Hogsmeade.”\n\n“We are walking.”\n\n“We are shopping.”\n\n“For necessary materials.”\n\n“We have three unopened jars of asphodel at Hogwarts.”\n\nHis stare was magnificent.\n\nI leaned closer. “Are you nervous?”\n\n“No.”\n\nThe answer came too fast.\n\nSomething warm opened in my chest.\n\n“Good,” I said, letting him keep the lie. “Because I am.”\n\nThat startled him.\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because I asked a man I like to spend time with me outside work, and he has spent the last half hour interrogating herbs to avoid acknowledging it.”\n\nHis gaze dropped briefly to my mouth.\n\nThen back to my eyes.\n\n“I am not interrogating herbs.”\n\n“You accused that valerian of fraud.”\n\n“It was mislabeled.”\n\nI smiled. “Terrifying.”\n\nHe bought nothing.\n\nOn the way out, he held the door for me and muttered, “This was not entirely unpleasant.”\n\nFrom Severus, it felt dangerously close to flowers.\n\nMy new boots did not leak at all.\n\nThe following week, another note appeared on my desk in the potions annex.\n\nYOUR UMBRELLA REMAINS IN MY OFFICE.\n\nBeneath it, in smaller handwriting, Severus had added:\n\nIT IS AN OFFENSE TO ENGINEERING.\n\nI went to retrieve it. He was sitting behind his desk with a book open, pretending not to notice my arrival.\n\n\"Do you make a hobby of insulting rain equipment?\" I asked.\n\n\"Only equipment that deserves it.\"\n\nI folded the note. \"There's something I need to tell you before either of us starts mistaking these little excursions for something we haven't discussed.\"\n\nHis eyes lifted. He closed the book with one finger marking the page.\n\n\"I am listening.\"\n\n\"I'm ethically non-monogamous. I prefer polyamory. If I build a romantic relationship, I don't want it to depend on promising that love can only ever involve one person.\"\n\nHe went very still.\n\n\"That is not a proposition you have previously made.\"\n\n\"I know. I'm making sure you have the information before you decide whether you'd like one.\"\n\nHe studied my face with the expression he reserved for a potion that had changed color unexpectedly.\n\n\"I presume the word ethical is doing a considerable amount of work.\"\n\n\"It should. It means actual consent. No hidden partners. No borrowing someone else's time without asking. No pretending a person who says no is cruel.\"\n\n\"And privacy?\"\n\n\"Agreed with the people involved. I would never talk about your history just because someone else is curious.\"\n\n\"What if I find an arrangement intolerable?\"\n\n\"Then I expect you to say so. I don't want a relationship where either of us has to disappear.\"\n\nHe looked at the umbrella.\n\n\"You seem to have thought about this.\"\n\n\"I have. A lot.\"\n\n\"Unlike your purchase of that contraption.\"\n\nI nearly laughed. \"Severus.\"\n\nHis expression softened by one degree. \"I do not give consent on the basis of a pleasant afternoon in Hogsmeade.\"\n\n\"I'm not asking you to.\"\n\n\"But you have not frightened me away,\" he said. \"I would like to continue our rather catastrophic experiment in mutual company. If it becomes something more, I will tell you what I can actually agree to.\"\n\nMy throat tightened.\n\n\"That's all I wanted.\"\n\n\"Then take your umbrella.\"\n\nI picked it up. He waited until I reached the door.\n\n\"Jasper.\"\n\nI turned.\n\n\"Thank you for asking me to choose instead of assuming what I would choose.\"\n\nFor Severus, that was almost a confession.\n\nI went into the rain with a broken umbrella and a remarkably steady heart.","word_count":2051},"after-always-42":{"number":42,"title":"Not an Appointment","content":"Iroh invited Severus Snape to tea by sending him a formal card.\n\nIt was cream-colored, folded once, and bore a small green dragon stamped in the corner. The handwriting inside was beautiful.\n\nDear Professor Snape, it said. Your company would be welcome at the Jasmine Steps tasting on Thursday. No speeches are required.\n\nSeverus read the card twice.\n\nThen he put it facedown on the kitchen table.\n\n\"Absolutely not.\"\n\nI was cutting an apple into pieces small enough to eat while sorting a stack of invoices. I did not look up.\n\n\"Are you declining the tea or the lack of speeches?\"\n\n\"The implication.\"\n\n\"Which implication?\"\n\n\"That I have become a public exhibit.\"\n\nI set down the knife.\n\n\"He's inviting you to drink tea.\"\n\n\"He is inviting the notorious surviving spy of the war into a room full of people who think an interesting guest constitutes an evening's entertainment.\"\n\nI folded the card and looked at the back. Nothing there suggested a lecture, a public question period, or a dramatic unveiling of Severus's biography.\n\n\"You could just say no,\" I said.\n\n\"I did.\"\n\n\"Then I'll tell him.\"\n\nSeverus turned toward me. He seemed almost disappointed by how easily I'd accepted his refusal.\n\n\"You intend to attend?\"\n\n\"Yes. I like tea.\"\n\n\"An insufficient reason to subject yourself to crowds.\"\n\n\"I also like Iroh.\"\n\nA small movement at the corner of his jaw told me he'd heard the choice of words. I gave him time to decide whether he wanted to ask the question behind it.\n\nHe did not.\n\nI went back to my apple.\n\nThat was not the end of the argument.\n\nIt followed us through breakfast, into the annex, and all the way to Moonroot Archive. Severus insisted he was not offended. He was merely unimpressed by the presumption of strangers who thought they were entitled to his time.\n\nI agreed that strangers were not entitled to his time.\n\nHe seemed to find this aggravating.\n\nNear noon, Iroh arrived in the archive carrying a basket of clean cups for the pantry. He greeted Neri, complimented Pip on a repaired door latch, and came toward our worktable with a smile.\n\nIroh set the basket down and checked Mina's loan sheet. The count matched. He had brought back every cup that the pantry had agreed to lend.\n\nSeverus watched him with the suspicion he usually reserved for mislabeled ingredients.\n\n\"Are you delivering crockery or an argument?\" he asked.\n\n\"Only the crockery, Professor. The argument would require a separate appointment.\"\n\nI nearly laughed. Iroh's expression was pleasant, but not empty; he had the quick eyes of someone who had once made plans under much worse conditions than a crowded archive.\n\nNeri signed for the cups. Iroh thanked them and folded the receipt. He didn't ask to see the historical records or make a claim on the workspace because he'd brought refreshments.\n\nSeverus tapped his pen against the table. \"I presume this is not a surprise inspection.\"\n\n\"Not unless you have been brewing tea without a permit.\"\n\n\"There is no such permit.\"\n\n\"Excellent. Then we have avoided another form.\"\n\nMina's numbers remained on the cloth, and the cups went to their owners. Even a small gift or invitation, I thought, could arrive with its proper limits intact.\n\n\"I hope the card reached you,\" he said.\n\n\"It did,\" Severus answered.\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"I shall not be attending.\"\n\nIroh bowed his head slightly.\n\n\"Then I hope we may meet another day.\"\n\nSeverus looked as though someone had misplaced the rest of his argument.\n\n\"That is all?\"\n\n\"Would you prefer a lengthy attempt to change your mind?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then I shall spare us both.\"\n\nIroh turned to me.\n\n\"I will save a cup for anyone who arrives before the kettle cools. If you have time, Jasper, you are welcome.\"\n\n\"I'll be there.\"\n\n\"Wonderful.\"\n\nHe left the basket behind and went to help Mara set up the afternoon pantry shelves.\n\nSeverus stared after him.\n\n\"He is either genuinely polite or very skilled at appearing so.\"\n\n\"Both could be true.\"\n\n\"I dislike uncertainty.\"\n\n\"I've noticed.\"\n\nThe rest of the day was ordinary in ways that became important only later. We cataloged three damaged journals. Severus corrected a mislabeled vial and did not insult the apprentice who had made the error. Neri brought a photograph of the underside of the well's old stone arch, and we compared it with the drawing from the washed-up ledger fragment.\n\nOne line on the old map was blank.\n\nSeverus marked it, but did not guess what it meant.\n\nIroh returned for the basket he'd left behind just as Neri brought a second copy of the washed-up ledger map.\n\n“Forgive the interruption,” he said. “Mara has discovered that an empty basket refuses to carry anything.”\n\nSeverus glanced at the basket. “A revelation for the ages.”\n\n“I thought so.”\n\nNeri spread the copy across the table. The blank line stopped just short of a dark mark near the lower maintenance passage. It might have been an erased signature. It might have been a damaged route label. A scholar had written in the margin that the mark showed where an old keeper had secretly opened the passage.\n\n“That is unsupported,” Severus said immediately.\n\nIroh leaned closer, hands behind his back. “If it were a route marker, would it not be drawn toward a place someone expected to reach?”\n\n“Only if we knew the mapmaker's notation. We don't.”\n\n“And if it were a signature?”\n\n“Then assigning an identity would be equally foolish.”\n\nI waited for Iroh to make an elegant pronouncement. Instead he studied the faint ink and frowned as though he were examining a battle map he no longer trusted.\n\n“In the war,” he said, “I have seen a general turn one uncertain line into a road because men were eager to march.”\n\nSeverus's expression changed. “A surprisingly rigorous observation.”\n\n“Do not encourage him,” I told Severus. “He'll want to illustrate it with tea leaves.”\n\n“I assure you,” Severus replied, “I require a specimen with a more predictable chemical structure.”\n\nNeri raised one hand.\n\n“Both of you. The ledger belongs to people who were denied the right to travel. I'd prefer our uncertainty be recorded before two clever men turn it into a contest.”\n\nThey were right. Severus made the annotation; Iroh signed as a reader who could confirm only what was visible on the copy. Neither claimed he knew the missing name. The original stayed protected, the lower passage stayed closed, and Neri decided which notes were suitable for public display.\n\nIroh collected his basket. “You would be welcome for tea another time, Professor.”\n\n“And you,” Severus answered, “would be welcome to examine a properly labeled map.”\n\nI nearly laughed. Neither of them seemed to realize that was their version of a friendly invitation.\n\n\nAt four o'clock, he closed his notebook.\n\n\"You should leave if you intend to be on time.\"\n\n\"Are you dismissing me?\"\n\n\"I am observing the clock.\"\n\n\"That is how you dismissed me yesterday.\"\n\n\"Then you have learned to recognize a pattern.\"\n\nI collected my bag and paused by the door.\n\n\"You could still come,\" I said. \"Not because anyone expects you to be charming. Just because you might like the tea.\"\n\nHe looked at me for a long moment.\n\n\"I would not like the people.\"\n\n\"Then enjoy your evening without them.\"\n\nHis expression softened slightly.\n\n\"Do not let them make you stay beyond your wishes.\"\n\n\"I won't.\"\n\nI went alone.\n\nJasmine Steps was crowded with the gentle noise of people who were not trying to impress one another. Greg had brought a guitar but was sitting with it unplayed across his knees. Katara was helping a neighbor carry a tray; Toph had occupied the chair with the best view of the garden despite not being interested in views.\n\nIroh poured a cup for me.\n\n\"Severus sends his regrets,\" I said.\n\n\"Does he?\"\n\n\"More accurately, he sends an exceptionally firm no.\"\n\n\"Then I am glad he was able to give it.\"\n\nWe drank the tea. It tasted of mountain mint and something warm beneath it that I could not name.\n\nAfter a while, Iroh asked how the archive was doing.\n\nI told him about the mislabeled recipe, the corrected copy, and Neri's old drainage diagrams. I did not tell him which pages Severus had kept private. It felt good to speak about our work without opening the parts of it that did not belong to me.\n\nGreg played a short melody after the cups were cleared. Toph complained that one note was half a beat late.\n\n\"That was on purpose,\" Greg said.\n\n\"Then you made a mistake intentionally. Congratulations.\"\n\nAang laughed so hard he nearly dropped his cup.\n\nI stayed until the lanterns came on, then walked back toward Moonroot alone. I could have stayed longer. I could have gone to another person's door. My relationships did not turn every independent evening into evidence for or against anybody. I wanted to go home.\n\nSeverus was in the kitchen at Spinner's End when I arrived.\n\nThe kettle had already been filled.\n\n\"How was the exhibition?\" he asked.\n\n\"Very disappointing. No one asked me to juggle, and nobody tried to turn me into a display.\"\n\n\"A shame.\"\n\n\"Greg played music.\"\n\n\"Predictable.\"\n\n\"Toph criticized the rhythm.\"\n\n\"An entirely reasonable response.\"\n\nI hung up my coat.\n\n\"Did you have a good evening?\"\n\n\"Quiet.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nHe looked at me.\n\n\"That is all you intend to ask?\"\n\n\"Did you want me to make an argument for attending next time?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then I won't.\"\n\nSeverus set two mugs on the table. He hesitated before speaking.\n\n\"I considered that you might interpret my refusal as disinterest in the people you care about.\"\n\n\"I didn't.\"\n\n\"Or as a demand that you decline invitations on my behalf.\"\n\n\"I wouldn't.\"\n\nHe took a breath.\n\n\"I prefer to know where I stand.\"\n\n\"You stand wherever you choose, Severus. You don't need to attend every gathering. And I don't need to pretend everyone I love has identical boundaries.\"\n\nSomething in his shoulders eased.\n\n\"I could perhaps tolerate a smaller visit,\" he said. \"At another time.\"\n\n\"Iroh would probably be delighted.\"\n\n\"Do not tell him I said delighted.\"\n\nI smiled.\n\nHe moved toward me, paused, and lifted a hand to my cheek. His touch was careful, but there was nothing uncertain about the way he looked at me.\n\n\"May I?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nThe kiss was brief, warm, and a little sharper when I smiled against his mouth. He rested his forehead against mine for one quiet beat.\n\nThe kettle began to whistle.\n\n\"That thing has the most appalling timing,\" I said.\n\n\"It has impeccable timing. You are simply distractible.\"\n\nThe tea was not as good as Iroh's.\n\nI told Severus so.\n\nHe informed me that my palate remained beyond repair.\n\nWe drank it anyway, together at a table where neither of us had been required to prove love by being anywhere we didn't wish to be.","word_count":1818}}};for(const s of lib.series){const dict=changes[s.series_slug]||{};for(const ch of s.chapters){const update=dict[ch.id];if(update&&update.number===ch.chapter_number){ch.content=update.content;ch.title=update.title;ch.word_count=update.word_count;}}}})();
